# Thesis 08 is the actual moat

The trust graph file is excellent conceptually because it rejects star ratings and instead records measurable outcomes: quote vs invoice, promised vs actual arrival, expected vs actual duration, cancellations and completion.

This is the thing I'd protect most carefully.

Eventually an agent should be able to ask:

```text
P(job completes successfully | supplier, SKU, postcode,
  property, requested date, quoted price)
```

Then supplier ranking becomes something like:

```text
expected_utility =
    success_probability
  × price_quality
  × availability_confidence
  × timing_accuracy
  × first_time_fix_probability
  - cancellation_risk
  - dispute_risk
```

That is vastly more valuable than:

> Jim Electrical — ⭐ 4.8/5

And it is extremely difficult for a competitor to copy because the data exists **only after transactions occur**.

