Yes. The thesis survives current verification, but the best version is slightly more precise:

> **Own the evidence-backed compatibility graph and live supplier state; use Merchant Center, Shopify Catalog, structured web data and eventually UCP/ACP as distribution rails.**

The feeds are extraordinarily well matched to this business, but **the feeds themselves are not the moat**. The moat is knowing, with unusually high certainty, *what the object is, what fits it, what does not fit it, what superseded it, and who actually has the correct replacement available now*.

Google's current Merchant Center schema strongly validates the direction. `related_product` is explicitly intended for conversational AI and supports `required_part`, `substitute`, `accessory`, `different_brand`, etc.; `product_detail` supports up to 100 structured details; Q&A supports up to 30 entries; manuals can be provided through `document_link`; and Google specifically recognizes the brand+MPN pattern for replacement/OEM parts that do not have GTINs. ([Google Help][1])

There is one important correction to `f66e7f6`: **`related_product` is not itself a general installed-base compatibility graph.** Google's relation points to other products in the merchant's inventory. So if you sell a Bosch pump but don't sell the 12-year-old Bosch dishwasher it fits, you cannot rely on `related_product` to express the crucial dishwasher→pump edge. That edge belongs in our canonical graph and gets flattened outward into `product_detail`, carefully chosen Q&A, manuals, PDP text/JSON-LD and images. `related_product` then represents relations among products we actually sell: old part→new part, substitute→substitute, required accessory, etc. ([Google Help][1])

