```

The ideal edge is not:

> MSZ-FH35 → remote X

if five competitors already explain it perfectly.

It's:

> photo + plate + error/problem → exact obscure lifecycle component.

That is where the information rent is.

## Automower is real, but I would use it as our benchmark corpus

Husqvarna's own catalog exposes multiple Automower power supplies—1A, 1.3A, 2.2A, 4.2A, 7A—with model fitment, while specialists publish superseded part numbers and year/model compatibility. Users still encounter confusing generation differences around chargers, stations, output ratings and older pinouts. ([Husqvarna][7])

That is excellent evidence that **compatibility pain is economically real**.

But it's also evidence that the seller data gap is narrower.

So I'd use Automower as a **benchmark**:

> Can our graph answer compatibility questions more accurately than Husqvarna + the best specialist?

If yes, our data engine is good.

Then deploy it in a market where the incumbent pages are much worse.

## Finland is becoming the obvious proving ground

There is an interesting confluence:

```text
~1.8m heat pumps
250k+ Allaway households
~3m saunas
large mechanical-ventilation installed base
long equipment lifetimes
Finnish-language documentation
excellent local logistics
lots of competent technical distributors
```

That is far more interesting for this thesis than generic “low ecommerce competition.”

It is **installed-base density × technical fragmentation**.

And the agent-distribution timing is real: Google's AI Mode currently supports both **Finland and Norway**, including Finnish and Norwegian language availability. ([Google Help][8])

There's a useful distinction on Shopify, though. Shopify Catalog can improve how products are represented to AI-shopping systems, but Shopify's direct **Sell on ChatGPT** channel currently requires the merchant to sell to U.S. customers. Therefore for a Finland-first store, I would prioritize:

```text
1. Google Merchant Center
2. excellent crawlable PDP/JSON-LD
3. Google Images/Lens readiness
4. Shopify as commerce backend + Catalog
5. ChatGPT direct channel when Nordic eligibility expands
6. UCP/ACP/MCP later
```

Shopify itself says eligible store products are automatically made available to Shopify Catalog for AI shopping, but inclusion does not guarantee that a product is selected in an answer. ([Shopify Help Center][9])

## There's another Google feature I would elevate: local inventory

Your phrase **“locally stocked”** can be represented directly.

Google's local inventory data supports store-level availability and lets merchants express whether a particular product exists at a particular location; Finland and Norway are among supported local-inventory markets. ([Google Help][10])

That means if a Finnish distributor has:

```text
Tampere: 6
Helsinki: 14
Turku: 2
```

our system should know this.

Even if we don't expose all those exact counts publicly:

```text
in_stock
local
dispatch_today
pickup_possible
supplier_confidence
```

is enormously useful agent state.

So supplier inventory synchronization is not a boring backend issue. **It is part of the recommendation advantage.**

## One further refinement to the feed strategy

Don't mechanically fill all 30 Q&As and 100 attributes.

Google explicitly advises avoiding redundant Q&A when the same answers exist in submitted documents, because it can extract information from those documents. ([Google Help][11])

The optimized pattern is:

| Surface               | Put here                                                             |
| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `product_detail`      | Deterministic structured facts                                       |
| `question_and_answer` | Ambiguities, negative compatibility, common human questions          |
| `document_link`       | OEM manual, installation guide, parts diagram                        |
| `related_product`     | Other products **we sell** that are substitutes/required/accessories |
| MPN/brand/GTIN        | Entity resolution                                                    |
| images                | Visual identity and differentiators                                  |
| video                 | Angles, connectors, model plate, physical recognition                |

Google added `video_link` in 2026 too, which is almost tailor-made for physical compatibility products because we can show the object, label, connector and mounting geometry. ([Google Help][12])

## The screening formula I'd now make canonical

Your previous research repeatedly showed why “few competitors” is the wrong metric. Some markets have few sellers because there's no demand; others have many sellers but all are terrible at solving the decision.

So I would score:

```text
HIGH_CERTAINTY_COMMERCE_SCORE =

installed_base
× replacement_frequency
× identity_difficulty
× compatibility_complexity
× supersession_complexity
× wrong_part_cost
× purchase_urgency
× supplier_stock_depth
× supplier_operational_quality
× digital_merchant_gap
× language_fragmentation
× visual_identifiability
× gross_margin
× shipping_suitability

÷ OEM_DTC_quality
÷ best_specialist_quality
÷ return_risk
÷ installation/safety_risk
```

And I'd make **BEST_SPECIALIST_QUALITY** a killer feature of the screening system.

Not:

> How many sellers?

But:

> **Can we materially beat the single best seller on certainty?**

If Innoair already gives the exact Mitsubishi mappings, plate-photo support and local stock, kill it.

If Onninen has the SKU but requires a trade login and doesn't solve a 1991 Allaway consumer's identity problem, attack it.

That distinction is the alpha.

## Supplier requirements become Priority Zero

I agree that feeds are the implementation priority on the demand/distribution side.

But before a niche can go live, I'd force a supplier gate:

```text
stock access                PASS
stable supplier SKU         PASS
wholesale economics         PASS
blind/direct shipping       PASS
dispatch SLA                PASS
returns/RMA                 PASS
stock freshness             PASS
consumer-safe packaging     PASS
```

Because the business has **no fulfillment operations**, its reputation is entirely downstream of suppliers.

And there is a legal distinction worth preserving: if the customer buys from **our store**, supplier dropshipping does not mean we cease being the retailer. EU distance-selling rules still generally give consumers withdrawal rights and statutory remedies against the seller for faulty goods. ([European Union][13])

So:

> no warehouse, yes.
> no shipping operation, yes.
> no inventory capital, yes.
> no retail responsibility, no.

That needs to be priced into margins.

# What I would launch first

**Finland → Allaway legacy central-vacuum compatibility.**

Not because central vacuums are exciting. Precisely because they aren't.

It has essentially every desired feature:

```text
boring
old
large installed base
model-dependent
visual identity possible
many aliases/generations
consumables + failures + upgrades
small parcel goods
local stock
trade-oriented distribution
repeat demand
high informational value
low fashion risk
```

Build 50–100 SKUs insanely well instead of 5,000 mediocre SKUs.

Then run **Vallox legacy ventilation** through the same engine.

Then **Norwegian cabin water**.

If those three work, the reusable company is obvious:

```text
NICHE DISCOVERY
      ↓
OEM DOCUMENT INGEST
      ↓
ASSET/PART GRAPH
      ↓
VISUAL IDENTIFICATION DATASET
      ↓
SUPPLIER NORMALIZATION
      ↓
GMC + SHOPIFY + WEB
      ↓
TRANSACTION
      ↓
WRONG-PART / SUCCESS OUTCOME
      ↓
GRAPH IMPROVES
      ↓
CLONE INTO NEXT INSTALLED BASE
```

That is dramatically better than conventional dropshipping because every launch leaves behind a reusable **resolution engine**, and each sale produces compatibility evidence rather than merely revenue.

The newest thesis has therefore sharpened the earlier one substantially: **we aren't searching for products with an ad arbitrage. We're searching for broken information markets attached to large physical installed bases.** That's a much more defensible search space.

